<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khurshid of Tabaristan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 761</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khurshid was the last Dabuyid ispahbad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, ruling from about 740. After the Abbasid revolution he paid tribute to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, but he rebelled in the late 750s, and in 759–760 Abbasid armies overran Tabaristan. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> and took poison around 761, ending the dynasty's rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khurshid-of-tabaristan.jpg" alt="Khurshid of Tabaristan" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ispahbod_Xurshid%27s_coin-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khurshid was born in 734 or 735, the son of Dadhburzmihr and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhan-the-great">Farrukhan the Great</a>, the first Dabuyid ruler from whose reign coins are known. By the traditional account the Dabuyids had made themselves autonomous rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> in the 640s, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Muslim conquest of Persia</a>, paying only tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and relying on their country's rough terrain. The historian P. Pourshariati holds instead that Farrukhan founded the family's rule in the 670s. Earlier scholars, misreading the coins, called him Khurshid II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Khurshid succeeded his father at the age of six, and his uncle Farrukhan-i Kuchak ruled as regent for eight years. When Khurshid came of age, Farrukhan's sons tried to seize the throne; a slave girl, Varmja Haraviya, is said to have betrayed their plot, and with the help of his cousin Jushnas's sons Khurshid imprisoned them and later married her. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-isfandiyar">Ibn Isfandiyar</a> describes a prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and other textiles and trading with the Turks, and Khurshid is said to have built many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-fitna">Third Fitna</a> he rebelled against Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a> and in 746 sent an embassy to the Tang court, which recognised him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> prince.</p><p>Forced to submit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a>, he backed the rebellion of Sunbadh after Abu Muslim's murder in 755. Sunbadh was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> by one of Khurshid's cousins, possibly at Khurshid's prompting, in the hope of gaining Abu Muslim's treasure. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mansur pressed him to give it up and raised the annual tribute to the level once paid to the Sassanids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the generals Abu al-Khaṣīb Marzuq and Khazim bin Khuzaymah took the fortress of al-Tak, Khurshid's family was carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. His daughters became concubines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, one of them of al-Mansur himself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> became a province ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amul</a>, though the Bavandids, Karinids and Zarmihrids kept the mountains as tributaries. Coins bore Khurshid's name until 764, which is why some works date his death to 767.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khurshid of Tabaristan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last ispahbad, <b>Khurshid</b>, rebelled against the Abbasid Caliphate, and in 759 to 760 Abbasid armies overran Tabaristan.</blockquote><a href="/state/dabuyid-dynasty">Dabuyid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sari-iran">Sari</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Deylam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.57,53.06&amp;year=751" aria-label="Sari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Khurshid of Tabaristan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4524 4425h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4524 4425h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.57,53.06&amp;year=751">What was here in 751 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
